Thus, to cover a set of positions `P` ⊆ `[l-1, r]`, for each `p in P`, there must be a token at `p` or `p+1` assigned to cover `p`.

This is a matching: tokens on left (positions `l..r`) can cover positions `l-1..r`, with token `i` covering `{i-1, i}`.

This is a bipartite graph where left nodes are tokens at `i=l..r`, right nodes are positions `j=l-1..r`, edge from token `i` to `j` iff `j = i-1` or `j = i`.

This graph is a path: token `i` connected to `i-1` and `i`, so positions `j` connected to tokens `j-1` and `j`.

A set `P` of `k` positions can be covered if the bipartite graph has a matching covering `P` (i.e., for `P`, each `j in P` has at least one incident token).

But since we pick `k` positions from `k+1` positions (`[l-1, r]` has `k+1` positions if `l>0`), and tokens are `k`, by Hall's theorem, it should be feasible: for any subset of `t` positions in `P`, the number of tokens incident to them is at least `t` because the graph is a path (no gaps), so yes, any `k` positions from `k+1` consecutive positions can be covered by `k` tokens on the path.

For example, `[l-1, r]` with `l-1, ..., r` (k+1 positions), tokens at `l, ..., r` (k tokens), each token `i` covers `i-1, i`, so token `l` covers `l-1, l`, token `r` covers `r-1, r`, etc.

To cover `k` positions out of `k+1`, it's always possible: if we skip `j`, we can assign token `j` or `j-1` to cover the others; formal but based on examples it works.

Thus, the maximum sum for a run `[l, r]` is the sum of the largest `k = r-l+1` values in `nums[l-1..r]` (if `l > 0`), else `sum(nums[l..r])` (since no `l-1`).

